WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:04.560
Hi.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.500
I'm Hadley Wickham, 
Chief Scientist at Posit

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.270
and for the purpose
of this video,

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.890
the chief maintainer
of the purrr package.

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:14.460
So what is the purrr package?

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:17.080
It's kind of hard to describe
what purrr is to be honest

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.840
but to put it into
words, I think

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.650
I've got a pretty good sense of like,
what function feels like a purrr

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:24.030
function to me.

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:25.770
But I think the
best way to think

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:30.270
of it is as a toolkit for
supporting, like, functional

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:32.159
programming.

00:00:32.159 --> 00:00:33.570
What is functional programming?

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.610
That's also kind of hard to explain.

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.040
The chief advantage is it
gives you a bunch of tools

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.090
for operating on vectors or
pairs of vectors or triplets,

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:47.190
et cetera, of vectors where you
work on each individual element

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:50.040
of the vector in isolation.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:54.330
And that allows you to apply
a function, to do something

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.498
to each element knowing
that there's no way

00:00:56.498 --> 00:00:58.290
that the other elements
are going to affect

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:00.480
the computation at all.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.620
So many of the functions of purrr
are alternatives to for loops.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.290
For loops by any
means, like, not a bad thing.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:10.230
You should never be embarrassed
or ashamed of using a for loop.

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:13.530
For loops are great because
they're very, very concrete

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:16.682
very obvious what you're doing
because you're doing, like, take

00:01:16.682 --> 00:01:19.140
this element and do this to
it, take that element, do that,

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:23.770
and you can very easily think
about, like, stepping through that.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:27.450
So the functions of purrr,
particularly the map functions

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:30.090
are kind of a step up in abstraction.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:36.300
And that step up is, it's a challenge.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.260
But I think there's some
good reasons to do it.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:42.000
And the first one is it
makes it easy to avoid

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.390
or ensures that you
avoid some performance

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:46.930
bottlenecks with loops.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:50.970
Now for loops themselves are
not slow, but if in your for loop

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:55.470
it's easy to get to accidentally
repeatedly modify or repeatedly

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:59.580
extend it, then can be slow,
although changes in the latest

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.560
versions of R have made that
much less bad than it

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:02.800
has been in the past.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:06.720
So performance, it's
a little bit useful.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.949
I think one big advantage
of switching to purrr

00:02:10.949 --> 00:02:19.210
is that you can also
switch to, oops not future, but the furrr package.

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:25.080
So the furrr package has exactly
the same syntax as purrr,

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:28.740
but it spreads a computation
across multiple points.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:33.930
So purrr, because it guarantees
that all of the computations

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:35.700
are independent
much, much easier

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.320
to share that work
across multiple calls.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:41.945
The other thing, particularly
in this latest version of purrr

00:02:41.945 --> 00:02:46.750
is that it gives you a really,
access to a really powerful and

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:49.280
useful tool, and that's
progress bars, which

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.630
I'll talk about a bit later.

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:54.200
But purrr is definitely kind of an
advanced programming tool.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.670
You can go a very long
way with for loops.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:03:00.110
But I think if you can
master the map functions with purrr,

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:05.210
you can write code that is more
succinct, more clear, and more

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:08.190
likely to be correct the
first time you write it.

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:11.210
So today, I'm going to
talk about purrr 1.0.

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:14.120
And the 1.0 release
of any package

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:17.780
is pretty special because
this kind of marks the package

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:20.540
at least, the initial
development is done.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:23.960
This is a package that
we are confident about.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:29.210
It's stable, and we expect it
to be around for the long-term.

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:30.740
And for purrr, in
particular, this

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:34.407
is an opportunity to take a
look at it broadly and say,

00:03:34.407 --> 00:03:35.990
which of the functions
in this package

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:38.360
are really related
to the core purpose

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:41.690
of functional programming,
of working with vectors

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:45.140
and functions, and
which functions of purrr

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:48.530
are not and maybe need to
be superseded or duplicated.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:51.170
So why don't we start with
some of my favorite features

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:53.990
of the purrr 1.0.

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:57.440
And I think the most exciting
feature or the coolest feature

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:58.760
is the new progress bar.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:02.230
So let's just make a very
silly little example.

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:04.220
So let's take a brief
look at what life

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:05.520
is like without progress bar.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.900
So let's just imagine we're
going to map over 100 numbers.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:12.410
And in lieu of doing
anything useful here,

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:14.230
I'm just going to sleep this.

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:17.690
I'm going to pause for
a tenth of a second.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.675
So I run this code.

00:04:19.675 --> 00:04:21.050
I know if something
is working, I

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:23.120
know this is going to
take 10 seconds of total,

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.640
but I'm getting like no feedback
on what's going on.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.750
And for a real life job,
you don't know exactly how

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:31.340
long it's going to
take, and you don't

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.530
know when something's
happening, like should you just

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:36.080
take a deep breath, should
you go grab a coffee,

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:38.750
or should you come
back tomorrow.

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:40.130
So it's great now.

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:44.840
In the latest version of purrr is
can say dot progress equals true

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:47.750
and get a convenient
progress bar that tells you

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:51.800
both how far through the process
you are and estimated time

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:55.370
to completion assuming that
every single iteration takes

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:56.690
about the same amount of time.

00:04:56.690 --> 00:05:00.110
So, that alone I think is pretty
great for interactive usage.

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:04.610
If you are putting these inside
of a function, you can add here instead of

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:09.260
just a true, you
can add a string,

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:12.650
which will be used as a
label for the progress bar

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:15.720
so you know exactly
what's going on.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:16.350
OK.

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:21.050
So my next really useful
feature inside of purrr

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:25.920
a big push across the tidyverse
is better error messages.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.110
Here again, I'm just going
to make up some dumb sample

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:32.262
data I've just taken.

00:05:32.262 --> 00:05:34.220
The numbers between and
one in 500 and randomly

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:35.450
reorganized them.

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:38.930
And I'm going to
make a little function.

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:45.200
And this function is
most of the time

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.840
it's just going to
double that number,

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:50.750
but every now and then that's
going to have an arrow.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:55.190
Again, I'm just kind of simulating what
might happen in your real code,

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:57.710
not particularly useful.

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:02.570
But now if I run this
code, what's really handy

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:04.670
is map is going to
tell you exactly

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:06.510
where that error occurred.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:09.800
So I can look at
the 318 argument,

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:13.220
and I know that's 123 which
by inspection of this function

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:14.720
is pretty easy to determine.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.750
But in real life this is really
helpful in like figuring out exactly

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:20.450
where that problem occurred.

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:22.310
Now, of course,
you can still use

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:26.600
all of the useful
functions in purr safely.

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:30.630
And possibly if
you expect errors,

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:34.370
you want to capture them
some way but this new ability

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:37.280
to tell you where the
error occurred, is hopefully

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:39.620
something that's helpful
and just make your life

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:41.670
a little bit easier every day.

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:43.130
We've also generally like
just reviewed

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:44.540
the error messages elsewhere.

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:46.940
Again, purrr has big
tidyverse effort

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:48.785
to make sure you get
better error messages.

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:56.340
Is it somewhere I can
show that to you here?

00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:01.280
So maybe if I try and supply
an environment or something

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:03.600
weird instead of a function.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:06.020
Now we have this pretty
standard kind of error framing

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:08.820
where we try and tell
you what went wrong,

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:11.960
it was an environment, what
did we want a function.

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:13.640
And you'll see
this more and more

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:17.330
as we work on improving error
messages across the tidyverse.

00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:21.410
We also have a new map
function called map_vec.

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:23.310
So if you're an
existing purrr user,

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:26.120
you probably know
about map_lgl produces

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:31.040
a logical vector, map_int
for integer vectors,

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:35.528
map_dbl for double vectors,
map_chr for character vectors.

00:07:35.528 --> 00:07:37.820
You might wonder like, what
about all the other things.

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:39.153
Is there going to be a map_date?

00:07:39.153 --> 00:07:41.150
Is there going to
be a map_factor?

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:43.040
Is there going to
be some kind of map

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:47.175
thing for every single
possible type of object in R?

00:07:47.175 --> 00:07:48.800
This is something
we've been struggling

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:50.060
with for a while in purrr.

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:52.680
We can never quite
figure out what to do.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:57.320
But there's now a new
function called map_vec

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:01.460
that's going to work with
any type of vector object,

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:04.220
so anything like a factor
or a date or a date time

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:05.430
is now going to work.

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:08.870
So for example, I could
map over the numbers 1 to 3

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:09.990
in my function.

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:12.290
I'm just going to add
those to today's date.

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:13.880
That's going to give
me another date,

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:18.380
and that's going to give
me a vector of dates.

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:27.730
Or I could take some letters,
first three letters, for the l,

00:08:27.730 --> 00:08:29.650
turn that into a factor.

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:34.480
And then when I use map_vec,
it's going to give me a factor,

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:36.039
just as you expect.

00:08:36.039 --> 00:08:39.070
So it uses the rules
from the vctrs package.

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:41.710
vctrs is a package
that hopefully

00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:44.240
you as a data scientist
don't need to care about.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:46.450
But it's something that
we're using more and more

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:49.930
in the depths of the tidyverse
to make sure all of the rules

00:08:49.930 --> 00:08:51.160
that we use are consistent.

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:53.530
So you don't have to
memorize these rules.

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:56.830
But hopefully, as you use
functions around the tidyverse,

00:08:56.830 --> 00:09:00.918
you just naturally over time
learn what those rules are.

00:09:00.918 --> 00:09:02.710
You might not be able
to make them explicit

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:05.230
but because everything
is consistent and behaves

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:09.220
in the same way, you build
up this internal knowledge

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:13.180
of the theory that
underlies these functions.

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:15.190
Now one thing I should
note about map_vec

00:09:15.190 --> 00:09:17.950
is one of its invariants,
one of its things

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:21.730
that always true is that
the length of the input

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:23.820
is the same of the
length of the output.

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:28.600
So if we try and create a new
vector, maybe I want to try

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:34.757
and just double every element,
that's not going to work.

00:09:34.757 --> 00:09:37.340
And again, I'm going to get this
error message telling me what

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:38.510
is supposed to happen.

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:40.670
Every element has
to be size one.

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:44.300
And in this case, I've got
an element that's size two.

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:50.970
So what happens if you do want
to do something like this?

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:53.280
Well, you can do this with map.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:57.870
That's going to give you a list
with each of those components.

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:02.040
And new in this version of
purrr are a set of functions

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:06.150
but list_c, list_rbind,
and list_cbind,

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:10.020
which lets you take
the output of functions

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:14.820
like this and for
example, combine them all into a vector.

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:16.840
Again, this is using
vctrs under the hood,

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:19.450
so this is going to give
you exactly the same.

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:21.360
It's going to use
exactly the same coercion

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:24.600
rules that map_vec uses.

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:28.950
But in this case, you can
use it to produce something

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:32.590
where every element
is a different thing.

00:10:32.590 --> 00:10:35.140
So for example, we
could take the element

00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:37.240
and repeat it the
number of times.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:40.410
So we'll get 1 1,
2 2, and 3 3's.

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:43.650
If we just map that, we can
see a list with those elements.

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:47.370
And then if we use list_c that's
going to concatenate them all

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:50.860
and goes into a single vector.

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:55.330
So list_rbind and
list_cbind works similarly,

00:10:55.330 --> 00:10:56.460
except for data frames.

00:10:56.460 --> 00:11:03.150
These kind of replace, these sort of supersede
map_dfr and map_dfc.

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:10.020
These functions
are super useful.

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:13.830
We know lots of people use
them, but they're not really

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:18.480
map functions because of
this idea of this invariant

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:22.830
that the input should be the
same length as the output

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:23.790
for a map function.

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:27.180
That is pretty important
for the meaning of this map.

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:29.580
And so we've decided these
functions aren't really

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:30.370
that great.

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:33.610
We've superseded them.

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:36.100
So if you're not familiar
with what that means,

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:38.350
that means they
are not going away.

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:39.660
They're not deprecated.

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:42.460
They're not on a path
to being removed.

00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:44.470
But we don't recommend
them anymore.

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:47.650
So in some sense these functions
are like the safest functions

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:49.650
to use because we're going
to just put them away

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:52.800
and we're never
going to touch them.

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:54.460
But we just don't
recommend using them.

00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:57.150
So instead we recommend
combining a map

00:11:57.150 --> 00:12:00.000
with one of these list
concatenation functions

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.390
where there's list_cbind,
list_rbind, or list_c.

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:06.450
So another area of purrr
that's got quite a lot of love

00:12:06.450 --> 00:12:08.220
in this release is
this general idea

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:13.440
of flattening and
simplification.

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:15.600
So this is about like what
happens if you've

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:18.780
got some complicated
nested list and you

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:21.750
want to simplify things.

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:24.180
And things have changed.

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:27.990
Since we wrote this stuff in
purrr, quite a lot of things

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:33.150
have changed, in particular
for example tidyr now has

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:37.230
some really nice tools like
unnest_longer and unnest_wider

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:42.870
so that if you are working with
a very hierarchical data set,

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:44.550
you've got something
from adjacent API

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:47.010
and you want to turn
it into a data frame,

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:50.640
these tidyr functions are
really the place to start now.

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:54.540
Previously, you had to use these
quite low level purrr functions.

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:56.370
You can still keep using those.

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:58.685
But generally, unnest_longer
and unnest_wider

00:12:58.685 --> 00:13:00.060
are going to allow
you to express

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:04.150
what you want a little bit more
simply and a little more clearly.

00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:07.890
But this gave us an opportunity
to look at these functions

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:11.310
in purrr and think now, 

00:13:11.310 --> 00:13:14.760
you're not being
forced to use them

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:17.670
for these data science
operations, the analysis

00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:19.170
operations.

00:13:19.170 --> 00:13:22.688
Now we can kind of think about what
are the right primitives here.

00:13:22.688 --> 00:13:25.230
These are things you're probably
not going to use as much day

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:25.890
to day.

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:28.170
They're more useful
when you're programming.

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:29.670
They might be tools
that we're going

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:32.620
to use elsewhere in the
tidyverse for solving problems.

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:36.120
But we wanted to take a
look holistically and think

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:36.960
what's going on.

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:41.460
And certainly, we cause a
lot of confusion in our team.

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:43.290
We couldn't remember
what does flatten

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:45.120
do, what does simplify do?

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:47.460
We've had some internal
functions and other packages

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:49.950
which do other things
and we just ended up

00:13:49.950 --> 00:13:51.360
causing a lot of confusion.

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:53.730
We didn't have a shared
language that we could

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:56.140
use in talking to one another.

00:13:56.140 --> 00:14:01.380
So we tried to unify
things, simplify things

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:02.730
in this version.

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:04.930
And just to give
you one example,

00:14:04.930 --> 00:14:08.740
let's talk about the
flattening function.

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:11.790
So here I have a list.

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:14.640
The first element of the
list is just a number.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:18.480
The second element of a
list is itself another list,

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.670
and the second element of
that list is another list.

00:14:21.670 --> 00:14:26.780
So we have this complicated
tree-like structure.

00:14:26.780 --> 00:14:30.220
Now what we can do
is use list_flatten

00:14:30.220 --> 00:14:33.530
to remove one layer
of that hierarchy.

00:14:33.530 --> 00:14:34.480
So let's just call it.

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:36.085
I'm going to use
str, again, just

00:14:36.085 --> 00:14:37.960
to illustrate the
structure a little bit more

00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:39.580
easily and then
see what happens.

00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:44.700
So just give a little bit
more room in the console,

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:46.550
so we can compare them.

00:14:46.550 --> 00:14:48.560
Previously, we had
a list two things.

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:51.200
Now we've got a
list of four things.

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:53.000
You can kind of see what's happened.

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:58.280
This list, the sub
list of three items

00:14:58.280 --> 00:15:01.980
is being pulled up
to the top level.

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:04.580
So that list itself
had another list on it,

00:15:04.580 --> 00:15:09.830
so we could call a list_flatten
again to flatten that.

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:14.960
And now we have like a, what we call
a perfectly flat list, a list

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:17.690
that doesn't contain
any other lists.

00:15:17.690 --> 00:15:20.747
And if we call
list_flatten on that again,

00:15:20.747 --> 00:15:21.830
nothing's going to happen.

00:15:21.830 --> 00:15:23.370
We're still going to get a list.

00:15:23.370 --> 00:15:27.110
So the list_flatten is always
going to return a list.

00:15:27.110 --> 00:15:29.240
You don't know how long
that list is going to be.

00:15:29.240 --> 00:15:31.100
It's going to be the sum.

00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:33.350
It's normally going to
increase in size because you're

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:35.558
going to have sub lists of
varying number of children

00:15:35.558 --> 00:15:39.290
that are going to get
hoisted up into the parent.

00:15:39.290 --> 00:15:44.890
But you have made this
list as flat as possible.

00:15:44.890 --> 00:15:48.030
Now if you want to
make this simpler,

00:15:48.030 --> 00:15:50.100
you can't make this
list any flatter,

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:54.480
but we could use a
simpler data structure.

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:56.910
If we call
list_simplify now, we're

00:15:56.910 --> 00:15:59.940
going to go to a simpler data
structure, which in this case

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:03.210
is a numeric vector.

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:06.000
So let's just illustrate the
difference between flattening

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:10.860
and simplification
with a list that

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:15.080
has a variety of types on that.

00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:17.570
So the same structure
as before, but now

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:21.770
we've got a logical in there
and a character vector.

00:16:21.770 --> 00:16:25.230
Let's flatten it.

00:16:25.230 --> 00:16:26.560
Look at the results.

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:28.380
So we've still got a list.

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:29.475
We'll flatten that again.

00:16:33.310 --> 00:16:35.290
We still have a list.

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:39.680
Now can we make
this any simpler?

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:42.710
Well, what happens if
we call list_simplify?

00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:45.400
Well, no, we can't make this
any simpler because we're

00:16:45.400 --> 00:16:51.430
trying to combine a double
and a character, a number

00:16:51.430 --> 00:16:52.900
and a string
together, and there's

00:16:52.900 --> 00:16:55.000
nothing simpler than
a list that will

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:59.630
allow you to have both of these
in a single data structure.

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:01.540
So this is as simple as we get.

00:17:01.540 --> 00:17:04.660
In this idea, now we've
got this clear distinction

00:17:04.660 --> 00:17:07.210
between flattening, which
is decreasing always.

00:17:07.210 --> 00:17:09.790
When you flatten a list,
you still get a list,

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:11.829
but the number of items
might have changed.

00:17:11.829 --> 00:17:13.960
And simplification,
we're going to always get

00:17:13.960 --> 00:17:19.359
something that's atomic
vector, or a factor, or a date.

00:17:19.359 --> 00:17:22.089
But you're always going to have
the same number of items in it.

00:17:22.089 --> 00:17:24.220
I think it has
this nice symmetry,

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:28.000
either you're changing the size
but keeping the type the same

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:33.340
or you're changing the type
and keeping the size the same.

00:17:33.340 --> 00:17:36.280
And that is a nice
split that hopefully

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:38.680
allows you to solve problems
without getting confused

00:17:38.680 --> 00:17:41.270
between what
different function do.

00:17:41.270 --> 00:17:44.620
So to finish up, I wanted
to cover what's possibly

00:17:44.620 --> 00:17:48.610
the biggest set of changes to
purrr in this release, which

00:17:48.610 --> 00:17:50.860
is basically saying
these are all the things

00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:54.250
that purrr no longer does,
that we don't believe

00:17:54.250 --> 00:17:57.940
that these are actually related
to the core purpose of purr.

00:17:57.940 --> 00:18:01.720
And I think this is a really
important part of the lifecycle

00:18:01.720 --> 00:18:04.210
of packages, that
as we understand

00:18:04.210 --> 00:18:06.880
what the core purpose of
the package is better,

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:08.890
we can remove things.

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:12.190
And that comes at a cost.

00:18:12.190 --> 00:18:13.960
If you're an existing
user, you might

00:18:13.960 --> 00:18:16.480
love some of these functions.

00:18:16.480 --> 00:18:18.670
That refinement
hurts existing users,

00:18:18.670 --> 00:18:21.010
but we do it for
the future users

00:18:21.010 --> 00:18:23.860
because when people in
the future come to purrr,

00:18:23.860 --> 00:18:26.680
they're going to see a
more tighter, more cohesive

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:29.630
set of functions that
are easier to understand.

00:18:29.630 --> 00:18:32.770
So most of these,
we have superseded.

00:18:32.770 --> 00:18:36.770
There are some things that we've
deprecated because we think

00:18:36.770 --> 00:18:38.410
they're pretty
uncommon to use them

00:18:38.410 --> 00:18:40.250
or there's some major
problems with them.

00:18:40.250 --> 00:18:42.250
So again, most of these
will continue to work.

00:18:42.250 --> 00:18:45.040
Superseded functions will
stay around for a long time.

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:48.910
Deprecated functions are
definitely on the path out,

00:18:48.910 --> 00:18:51.890
but we're trying to do that
as gradually as possible.

00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:55.030
So if you use a deprecated
function and in the first release

00:18:55.030 --> 00:18:58.240
it's deprecated, it'll
warn you like every 8 hours

00:18:58.240 --> 00:19:00.190
that that function's going away.

00:19:00.190 --> 00:19:06.310
We're trying to walk this line
between being annoying enough

00:19:06.310 --> 00:19:09.310
that you eventually get rid
of the deprecated function

00:19:09.310 --> 00:19:12.400
in favor of the new
solution, but not so annoying

00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:15.460
that it's as bad as just
taking the function away.

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:18.440
So deprecated functions
in this release,

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:20.890
they will warn every 8 hours.

00:19:20.890 --> 00:19:26.230
In the next bigger release,
which should be in 1 or 2 years,

00:19:26.230 --> 00:19:29.290
they'll warn every
single time you use them.

00:19:29.290 --> 00:19:31.240
And then some release
after that, probably

00:19:31.240 --> 00:19:32.980
two or three years
after that, they

00:19:32.980 --> 00:19:35.180
will error when you use them.

00:19:35.180 --> 00:19:38.590
So deprecated function, you've
still got a couple of years

00:19:38.590 --> 00:19:41.110
to fix it, but
you really do need

00:19:41.110 --> 00:19:43.870
to work on that at some point
because those functions are

00:19:43.870 --> 00:19:44.560
their way out.

00:19:44.560 --> 00:19:49.210
So let's do a quick overview
though of those functions.

00:19:49.210 --> 00:19:51.610
I'm just looking
at the news file,

00:19:51.610 --> 00:19:55.100
which tracks every single change
you've made to the package.

00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:57.790
If you're a really
big user of a package,

00:19:57.790 --> 00:20:00.820
I think it's a really good
idea to familiarize yourself

00:20:00.820 --> 00:20:04.660
with these files because
in the blog post,

00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:07.720
we try and just hit the biggest
point, the things that we think

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:11.300
are most important, but useful
for you to scan the newest

00:20:11.300 --> 00:20:13.300
functions because things
that might be important

00:20:13.300 --> 00:20:16.540
for you might not be important
for lots of other people,

00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:18.100
or we might just be wrong.

00:20:18.100 --> 00:20:21.430
Also great, like if you've
got your own blog

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:24.070
and want to find something
to write about, read

00:20:24.070 --> 00:20:26.860
through the news of packages and
look at stuff that we haven't

00:20:26.860 --> 00:20:29.200
advertised because
I can guarantee you,

00:20:29.200 --> 00:20:31.690
there are useful blog posts to be written about things

00:20:31.690 --> 00:20:35.620
that we've changed that we're
not going to be writing about.

00:20:35.620 --> 00:20:38.140
And that's a great way to
dig into the development

00:20:38.140 --> 00:20:40.730
of a package and help
educate others as well.

00:20:40.730 --> 00:20:43.690
So quick summary,
cross, I think,

00:20:43.690 --> 00:20:45.208
it was a pretty old function.

00:20:45.208 --> 00:20:46.750
And I think many
people are using it,

00:20:46.750 --> 00:20:51.520
but you're much better off using
tidyr's expand_grid function.

00:20:51.520 --> 00:20:56.440
We had a bunch of functions that
used non-standard evaluation

00:20:56.440 --> 00:20:57.770
in some way.

00:20:57.770 --> 00:21:00.490
There was these just
three functions in purrr

00:21:00.490 --> 00:21:02.560
that use non-standard
evaluation or tidy

00:21:02.560 --> 00:21:06.590
evaluation in some way which
just felt like not a good fit.

00:21:06.590 --> 00:21:07.870
So we've got rid of those.

00:21:07.870 --> 00:21:12.190
And no function in purrr uses,
or no non-deprecated function

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:15.940
in purrr uses
tidy_select anymore.

00:21:15.940 --> 00:21:17.830
We have the lift family
of functions, which

00:21:17.830 --> 00:21:19.870
I think are a really cool idea.

00:21:19.870 --> 00:21:21.550
They're sort of a little
bit mind blowing

00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:23.675
about what you can do with
functions and functional

00:21:23.675 --> 00:21:24.380
programming.

00:21:24.380 --> 00:21:29.390
But it's just an unusual
style of programming.

00:21:29.390 --> 00:21:31.180
And we think that most
people are better

00:21:31.180 --> 00:21:34.210
off not knowing about it.

00:21:34.210 --> 00:21:36.680
Then we have a bunch of
functions that I'm like,

00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:39.490
I have no idea why
we ever included

00:21:39.490 --> 00:21:43.330
these in purrr, especially these
random number generators ones.

00:21:43.330 --> 00:21:45.970
I'm not sure why they
ever ended up in purrr.

00:21:45.970 --> 00:21:48.310
But they're now on the way out.

00:21:48.310 --> 00:21:53.050
And splice, which is yet
a much older tool that

00:21:53.050 --> 00:22:00.210
allows you to kind of cram
together like lists of lists.

00:22:00.210 --> 00:22:03.240
We used to use it as kind of
an alternative for some

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:05.640
of the stuff that we've
replaced with dynamic dots

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:06.720
or tidy-select.

00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:10.170
Those just doesn't
make any sense.

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:11.910
Other stuff in
maps, so things you

00:22:11.910 --> 00:22:16.633
could do with map
functions, but again, I

00:22:16.633 --> 00:22:18.300
don't know why we
ever implemented these

00:22:18.300 --> 00:22:20.050
because they're not
really vectors so they

00:22:20.050 --> 00:22:21.510
don't work anymore.

00:22:21.510 --> 00:22:23.010
We got rid of the raw variants.

00:22:23.010 --> 00:22:26.220
I don't think anyone
ever actually used them.

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:31.650
We've made some changes to
how things like map character

00:22:31.650 --> 00:22:32.520
works.

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:36.980
Previously, you could just
do something like this.

00:22:42.220 --> 00:22:45.220
And that would give
you a character vector.

00:22:45.220 --> 00:22:46.600
Now it's going to warn.

00:22:46.600 --> 00:22:49.270
And the reason we warn is
because the way it does

00:22:49.270 --> 00:22:52.330
the coercion is pretty weird.

00:22:52.330 --> 00:22:55.660
And I think this--

00:22:55.660 --> 00:22:56.700
I don't know if I can.

00:23:01.740 --> 00:23:02.910
It's pretty weird.

00:23:02.910 --> 00:23:04.860
So if you're creating
really small numbers,

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:07.180
it just like silently
truncates them,

00:23:07.180 --> 00:23:08.670
which is clearly pretty bad.

00:23:08.670 --> 00:23:09.960
So we got rid of that.

00:23:13.470 --> 00:23:15.420
There are bunch of other
deprecated functions.

00:23:15.420 --> 00:23:18.450
The previous releases are
matching along the process

00:23:18.450 --> 00:23:21.930
to going away altogether,
so a couple of functions.

00:23:21.930 --> 00:23:24.720
Here is two functions
that have gone away now

00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:27.600
that we deprecated
five years ago.

00:23:27.600 --> 00:23:29.220
Now we can finally
get rid of them.

00:23:33.880 --> 00:23:37.270
I think those are the main ones.

00:23:37.270 --> 00:23:41.440
I think part of the really
important part of the process

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:45.250
of package lifecycle
and package development

00:23:45.250 --> 00:23:47.240
is not just adding
new functions,

00:23:47.240 --> 00:23:50.470
but it is equally important
to remove those functions.

00:23:50.470 --> 00:23:53.830
And we know that removing
functions that used to work

00:23:53.830 --> 00:23:55.730
is frustrating.

00:23:55.730 --> 00:23:59.710
And so we have developed this
process, this lifecycle process

00:23:59.710 --> 00:24:03.640
of deprecation where it starts
with pretty mild warnings

00:24:03.640 --> 00:24:05.680
and gets progressively
aggressive, more

00:24:05.680 --> 00:24:07.300
and more aggressive over time.

00:24:07.300 --> 00:24:09.160
We hope this is useful.

00:24:09.160 --> 00:24:11.950
We hope that this
gradually pushes

00:24:11.950 --> 00:24:16.348
you towards the golden
path without forcing you rudely

00:24:16.348 --> 00:24:17.890
when you're working
on something else

00:24:17.890 --> 00:24:19.760
to urgently switch something.

00:24:19.760 --> 00:24:22.810
But at the same time,
we believe that we want

00:24:22.810 --> 00:24:24.100
these functions to be better.

00:24:24.100 --> 00:24:26.780
We want these things to
have a more consistent,

00:24:26.780 --> 00:24:28.240
cohesive syntax
because that makes

00:24:28.240 --> 00:24:30.370
it easier for future users.

00:24:30.370 --> 00:24:33.560
And I think we're
doing pretty well.

00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:34.990
We've certainly
got a lot better,

00:24:34.990 --> 00:24:37.780
I think, at handling
deprecation and breakages,

00:24:37.780 --> 00:24:40.420
but we're certainly still
trying, still learning.

00:24:40.420 --> 00:24:43.180
And if you have any
feedback, please let us know.

00:24:43.180 --> 00:24:45.430
If you're interested in
learning more about functional

00:24:45.430 --> 00:24:49.060
programming in general, I think
a really good place to start

00:24:49.060 --> 00:24:51.485
is Advanced R, which
is three chapters

00:24:51.485 --> 00:24:53.110
on functional
programming, functionals,

00:24:53.110 --> 00:24:56.020
function factories,
and function operators.

00:24:56.020 --> 00:24:58.415
And there's a lot of
text here, but you

00:24:58.415 --> 00:25:00.040
don't have to
necessarily read the text

00:25:00.040 --> 00:25:02.960
because I think it's got some
really good pictures as well.

00:25:02.960 --> 00:25:06.050
And this is kind of the basic idea
of a map function.

00:25:06.050 --> 00:25:07.090
You take a map.

00:25:07.090 --> 00:25:10.180
The first argument is a vector,
and you've got a function.

00:25:10.180 --> 00:25:12.190
And what that gives
you is a vector

00:25:12.190 --> 00:25:14.530
in the result
where you've called

00:25:14.530 --> 00:25:18.400
if on each element
of the vector in turn.

00:25:18.400 --> 00:25:22.160
And once you've got that
idea, then we can scroll down.

00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:28.180
And what happens if you
have like a list of elements

00:25:28.180 --> 00:25:33.370
or going a little bit further,
what happens if you supply

00:25:33.370 --> 00:25:43.430
additional arguments or
what happens if you have

00:25:43.430 --> 00:25:46.850
multiple vectors that you want
to iterate through or iterate

00:25:46.850 --> 00:25:48.860
on parallel?

00:25:48.860 --> 00:25:51.485
I think these pictures give
you a really quick overview

00:25:51.485 --> 00:25:53.720
of what does map2 do.

00:25:53.720 --> 00:25:56.960
Well, map2 is two
vectors, and it

00:25:56.960 --> 00:25:59.015
calls if on the first
element of the first one,

00:25:59.015 --> 00:26:00.890
and the first element
of the second one, then

00:26:00.890 --> 00:26:03.510
the second element of
this one, and so on.

00:26:03.510 --> 00:26:06.050
And then once you've
internalized what map2 does,

00:26:06.050 --> 00:26:09.020
you might wonder, well, what
happens if it got three vectors

00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:11.420
or I care about the output?

00:26:11.420 --> 00:26:16.467
And I think if you just
skim through these pictures,

00:26:16.467 --> 00:26:18.050
then we can get into
the reduce family

00:26:18.050 --> 00:26:20.660
and other more complicated
things, this can give you

00:26:20.660 --> 00:26:24.330
a great sense of the basic
idea of these functions.

00:26:24.330 --> 00:26:30.690
So in this video, I've given you, the top highlights, my top highlights, of purrr 1.0.

00:26:30.690 --> 00:26:33.590
If you want to learn
more, read the blog post.

00:26:33.590 --> 00:26:36.260
If you want more
details than that,

00:26:36.260 --> 00:26:43.010
I'd recommend reading the news
file as I mentioned earlier.

00:26:43.010 --> 00:26:46.850
If you are using a
deprecated function

00:26:46.850 --> 00:26:48.950
or a superseded
function and want

00:26:48.950 --> 00:26:51.500
to learn what you
should do now, I

00:26:51.500 --> 00:26:53.480
forgot to mention this
earlier, but if you

00:26:53.480 --> 00:26:56.450
go to the documentation
for that function

00:26:56.450 --> 00:26:58.730
and scroll down to
the examples, you'll

00:26:58.730 --> 00:27:00.560
see that there's a
bunch of examples

00:27:00.560 --> 00:27:03.810
that show you what
did you do previously

00:27:03.810 --> 00:27:05.660
and what you should do now.

00:27:05.660 --> 00:27:10.043
So hopefully, that will guide
you in updating your code too.

00:27:10.043 --> 00:27:12.710
If you've got other problems, if
we have broken something that's

00:27:12.710 --> 00:27:17.510
really important to
you, a great place to go

00:27:17.510 --> 00:27:23.970
is the GitHub page for purr,
log your issues, log questions.

00:27:23.970 --> 00:27:26.240
If you do have a
problem with your code,

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:29.810
really, really useful for
us if you create a reprex

00:27:29.810 --> 00:27:32.000
or reproducible example.

00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:35.490
Of course, we have a
package to help you do that.

00:27:35.490 --> 00:27:37.700
So you might want to
learn a little bit

00:27:37.700 --> 00:27:38.960
about using reprexes.

00:27:38.960 --> 00:27:41.720
This just gives us
some runnable code

00:27:41.720 --> 00:27:43.590
that we can immediately run.

00:27:43.590 --> 00:27:46.400
There's no trying to figure
out exactly what you did.

00:27:46.400 --> 00:27:50.130
We can take your code, see
what you did, run it ourselves.

00:27:50.130 --> 00:27:54.433
And that just makes answering
your question that much easier.

00:27:54.433 --> 00:27:55.850
If you have something
that doesn't

00:27:55.850 --> 00:27:59.450
rise quite to the level of
a bug or a GitHub issue,

00:27:59.450 --> 00:28:03.500
another great place to
reach me is on Mastodon.

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:07.700
I'm very happy to answer
any general questions there.

00:28:07.700 --> 00:28:09.860
I'll point you in the
right direction to places

00:28:09.860 --> 00:28:11.720
in the docs.

00:28:11.720 --> 00:28:14.000
So I hope you've enjoyed
learning more about purrr,

00:28:14.000 --> 00:28:16.070
and thanks for
watching this video.

